        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Smithsonite is a zinc carbonate, a secondary mineral of the oxidation zone of zinc deposits. Near the surface, rainwater charged with carbon dioxide attacks sphalerite, the primary zinc sulphide, and the freed zinc recombines with the carbonates of the host limestones; smithsonite is then deposited as botryoidal crusts, stalactites and spongy masses. Its colours vary with impurities: blue-green (copper), pink (cobalt), yellow (cadmium), lavender, apple green or white. It is named after James Smithson, the chemist whose bequest founded the Smithsonian Institution.",
            "Trigonal system. Rarely as rhombohedral crystals; mostly botryoidal, reniform, stalactitic or in bony masses (“dry bone ore”). Perfect rhombohedral cleavage in crystals; uneven fracture. Hardness 4 to 4.5; specific gravity 4.3 to 4.5, heavy for a pale mineral; vitreous to pearly lustre, silky on botryoidal surfaces; translucent; refractive index 1.621 to 1.849; very strong birefringence, 0.228. Effervesces in warm acids. Soft, it is cut as cabochons and carvings, best worn as pendants rather than rings.",
            "Namibia: Tsumeb, pink cobaltoan and blue-green smithsonites among the finest in the world. United States: the Kelly Mine (Magdalena, New Mexico), famous turquoise-blue-green smithsonite, and Arkansas (yellow “turkey fat ore”). Mexico: Santa Eulalia and Choix (Sinaloa), pink and green smithsonites. Also Greece (Lavrion), Sardinia (Iglesiente), China (Yunnan), Zambia and Spain.",
            "A stone of gentleness and kindness, said to soothe emotions, ease tension and support one through grief or change. It is associated with the heart and throat chakras and the signs of Virgo and Pisces. Avoid prolonged water, acids and knocks; cleanse by smudging; recharge in moonlight."
